What you have here, IMHO, is a standard intended to prevent abuse.  It 
seems that whoever wrote the guidelines was not thinking about legitimate 
uses of privacy.  In my experience with U Chicago, this is not 
surprising.

It looks like this is a good faith attempt to avoid real abuses that 
happens to be constructed such to make it easy to trod on well intended 
attempts at anonymous posting, psuedo-posting, and alias posting.

The subjective standard "Concealing or misrepresenting your name or
affiliation to mask irresponsible or offensive behavior is a serious 
abuse." seems to be a wordy clause for "Maliciously misrepresenting your
name or affiliation is a serious abuse."

Nevertheless, that path to hell is paved and all that.

I would consider pointing out legitimate uses for anonymous posting and 
the like to the proper authority in the U Chicago Academic and Public 
computing department.  Despite the often reactionary attitude at U o C, 
I'll bet they change the wording when you bring up the liberal appeal of 
recovery groups on the net.  (No value judgement implied for those of who 
are reading liberal as an insult... No liberal flamers please...)  


