>>  affiliation to mask irresponsible or offensive behavior is a serious abuse.
>>  Using identifiers of other individuals as your own constitutes fraud.
>
>It is uncertain to me, on the basis of language elsewhere in this otherwise
>unexceptionable policy statement, whether the actions described above are
>considered actionable _per se_.  

MCSNet has no problem with <anonymous> messages, or pseudononymous messages,
<provided> that you are not perpetrating a fraud.  <Forging> messages so 
that they appear to have come from another system or person constitutes 
perpetrating a fraud.  This includes cancels for articles you did not 
originally post.

Anonymous postings <can> be construed as fraudulent, depending on the intent
of the transmission and how it is done.  If it looks to have come from a
legitimate user other than yourself then you're going to take heat for it
here.  Using anonymous posting services or "anonxxxx" is ok, as long as you 
remain clear of the other problem areas.

Harassment, in all its forms, is covered under other parts of our subscriber
agreement and is generally against the rules.

There is usually enough of a trail here that I can figure out who sent what
if I really need to.  So far it has not been necessary to use this
capability.

I would suggest that UofC take this policy and adapt it rather than try to
have something which can be easily perverted for witch-hunts.

