>Please show me where the Constitution guarantees the right to
>ANONYMITY, with the exception of the voting booth.

First of all, the Constitution doesn't explicitly guarantee either the
anonymity of the voting booth or the right to vote. (Look it up if you
don't believe me.)

Secondly, to the extent that the right to anonymity is linked to the right
to privacy, it can be said to be grounded in the First Amendment (see
NAACP v. Alabama, 1958, in which the Supreme Court held it
unconstitutional for Alabama state government to require the NAACP to
disclose its membership list).


