Subject: Internet Legalities Question

Is anybody aware of any laws that might govern or regulate the transmission
of Internet electonic mail into government computer facilities?

Say I have a friend who is a user on a U.S. government-owned mainframe
installation.  This installation is on the Internet.  I wish to send this
friend a "personal" message.  (By "personal" I mean that the subject of this
message is not related to my friend's job, which is why she has her account in
the first place.)  This is just a friendly "Hi. How-are-you?" type message.

Now, say this friend of mine has a co-worker who is a real Jerk.  This Jerk
finds out that I am sending personal messages to my friend and then tries to
say that my sending of these messages "into" this government computer is an
"unauthorized use" of that government computer and is therefore "against the
law".

Does anyone know if this is true or not?  Does my sending messages into their
facility constitute "unauthorized use" of their computer?  Or does simply
being a member node on the Internet give other Net users implied consent
to send messages there?

There have been court cases involving Email, but as I understand it, these
were mostly Invasion-of-Privacy issues.  I want to know if I am breaking
the law when the destination of my messaging is someone else's computer.

Comments please. Thanx.

Tim Smith
The American University
Washington, D.C.

