Just to add a bit more to the issue, the NAACP has contacted every
high school in Kentucky that uses the nickname "Rebels," pressuring
them to adopt new nicknames/mascots.

I certainly can't judge the outrage of others, but I have to wonder:
in light of the *major* problems we have in today's society, is this
stuff really *that* important?  One of my high school's local rivals
used the "Rebel" nickname/mascot; I've seen black students cheerlea-
ding and (in one instance) serving as the Rebel mascot!

A correspondent in rec.sport.basketball.college recently constructed
a "master list" of collegiate mascots.  If memory serves, there were
Minutemen, Rebels, Redskins, Braves, Seminoles, and many other "offen-
sive" nicknames.

If the students of that school don't have a problem with it, I'm not
sure that I can support the notion of some third party running in and
talking about "offense."  For instance, the Florida State University's
use of the Seminole mascot has the full blessing of the Seminole Nation;
who's going to tell the Seminole Nation that they're being offended?

With hungry/poor/illiterate/abused/addicted/sick kids out there by the
millions, we're wasting time/money/media-space on *high school mascots*?
Geez, talk about misplaced priorities...

--Wes

