  As I said, I was raised in Miami.  It isn't what you would consider
a very "Southern" city.  However, I think I've got enough "South" to
give another side of this argument.  For instance, my American History
teacher told us when we started discussing the Civil War that he would
try to be impartial but could not as his great-grandfather's farm was
burned down when Sherman marched through Georgia.  
  The impression I got from growing up in Miami (and not just what I
learned from the teacher) was that the War was over more than slavery
alone.  It was also about how to govern and live.  The North was more
industrialized, educated, and urban than the more agricultural South.
(In fact, slavery emphasized the difference).  I believe one of the
rallying cries of the South since the 1820's was "state's rights".
  So with the North getting more and more influential, Southerners
started getting worried that their culture, slavery and all, would
start to disappear.  (Hey!  Doesn't that sound familiar?  Maybe I _am_
part of a persecuted group :)   <--- note the smiliey )
  Thus, I was raised thinking that the South fought to keep political
control with the states, instead of the federal government, and that
the basis of the difference between the North and South arose from
cultural differences implicit in maintaining the despicible practice
of slavery.  Hence, the term "rebel" is taken more to mean someone
willing to fight to retain {their culture, the status quo, their
lifestyle; I don't the right word to use :( perhaps "way of life"?}
more than it refers to someone who fights to retain the slave trade.

  Thus, _I_ do not see the team name "rebel" offensive because _to_me_
the term "rebel", and its usage, describes someone willing to defend
their beliefs.  Again, bear in mind that I detest the concept of
owning people ("people as property"?; wish I had thought of that phrase
back in high school :) ), so I don't like most of the beliefs.
  Now, I can believe that a lot of the the people in Kentucky were
raised the same way I was, in that "rebel"<>"slaver".  To them, and to
some extent, me, taking away the team name "rebel" takes away part of
the cultural history of the South.  Their protest against its
withdrawl is (IMO) the natural opposite of the current trend of
attacking sports teams for using "Indian" mascots/ traditions/ icons/
phrases.

   In response to some of Prof. Froomkin's comments.  I too would be
horrified to hear of a team named after the Wehrmacht, or for that
matter named "Uber Alles".  All that does, though, is point out the
power of a name.  Undoubtably there are some people who would love the
team name "Wehrmacht".  Alas, this whole issue isn't easy to work out
and, I dare say, intractable.

   To finish it all up.  What are valid names to call sports teams?
So far I have heard that it is inappropriate to name them after any
Indian tribe (does that include Cadallic? :) ), after the Minute Men,
and the Rebels.  In high school I was a "Cavalier"; they were probably
sexist.  What about Trojans?  No, I think they too had slaves.  Is
there any "safe" name to use which refers to people?  How about "The
Senates"? :)  Or astronomical bodies! "The M-38s".  

