I can udnerstand why something like this would be potentially dangerous.  We
have enough problems with small, highly motivated groups throwing the results
of precinct caucuses as it is, without introducing a system like this.
Imagine, for example, if someone from some group were to post an article
advocating the death penalty for abortions.  This group (I think you all
can imagine who) then gets all its members to eVote in favor of it.  Since
only a very tiny fraction of the overall population would eVote (but a very
large* portion of the interested group), the results would be very highly
skewed in favor of the interest group.

In fact, I think that such a system would almost never* (in practice) serve
as a conduit for legitimate grass-roots opinions; but rather it would be
exploited by just about every special-interest group imaginable to lend some
degree of legitimacy to their latest cause.  In that sense, it would undermine
democracy by giving an excessively large voice to small but highly motivated
groups (after all, its pretty hard to ignore it when the system comes back
with "67% of voters favor the death penalty for abortion.").

